Bagels wrote:
> A question to everyone here: why do you play NetHack? 

It is of non-trivial complexity, to say the least.
You can still get new situations/challenges after playing for years.
You can still learn new things after many years of playing.
It has (mostly) an efficient interface concept (key input/text output).
A lot is configurable to personal preferences.
It has funny details (and "TDTTOE").
It has mostly non-static dynamic dungeons.
It is balanced, very well balanced!
It is hard to beat, but you can beat it.
You can play it in many ways, your own style.
You can orient your gameplay by real life intuition.
It is very well documented
It is under active development, even less significant bugs taken seriously
The program has a very high quality WRT stability and reliability.
You can play it a decade long and still enjoy.

> Particularly, why do you play it over servers (like nethack.alt.org)?

Some patches that increased my survivability a lot (colored HPs).
Games are recorded and replayable, end of game statistics collected.
Overall statistics available about everything (well, kind of).
You cannot cheat (thus not cheat yourself :-).
You can be observed during your play; so I play less like a daredevil.
A minor point for me: occasionally you get bones from other players.
Communicating with players through Nethack's scroll of mail interface.

> In addition, I'm particularly curious about is this: how have other 
> players helped you (or hindered you) in your games? 

Not often but occasionally a detail that is new to me is posted here.
Viewing selected games on NAO can inspire you to try out new things.

Some tactics and some strategies suggested here work only in specific
contexts or are highly depending on the playing style of the proposer;
one must be very careful if trying to adopt the suggestions. Nethack
has too many degrees of freedom and too many factors that can make it
dangerous to simply imitate any suggestion.

